diagnosis_text:
A full multi-layer sweep of the simple_bgp topology found no fault matching the reported symptom. Phase 1 reachability testing showed pc1 ↔ pc2 at 0% packet loss in both directions, directly contradicting the ticket claim. Phase A infrastructure triage (infra_sweep and l2_snapshot) found no ACL rules, no static ARP poison, no addressing anomalies, no duplicate MACs, and all interfaces UP. Phase B control-plane triage confirmed that both FRR daemons on router1 and router2 are fully operational (watchfrr, zebra, and bgpd all active), the eBGP session between ASN 1 and ASN 2 is in Established state, and router2 is actively advertising 200.1.1.0/24. Direct inspection of router1's FIB via `show ip route` confirms the BGP-learned entry `B>* 200.1.1.0/24 [20/0] via 193.10.11.2, eth0` is installed and selected. Router2 symmetrically holds `B>* 195.11.14.0/24` for the return path. TC snapshot found only default fq_codel qdiscs — no bandwidth throttle or corruption injection. Pressure sweep found no stress tools, CPU spikes, or socket exhaustion. The network team's hypothesis — that router2 stopped advertising its prefix and the route is missing from router1 — is factually incorrect at the time of investigation. No corrective action is required; the network is operating normally.
=== END ===